ound 82-89% to win", "Mendoza has outraised incumbent Ciscomani ($11.2M to $7.6M) in a favorable midterm environment", "Thin, dated, sponsor-leaning polling leaves real uncertainty about the margin"], "rationale": "Every major rater moves this even-PVI district toward a Democratic flip, and markets agree at roughly 85-89% for Mendoza, while she leads decisively on fundraising in what looks like a strong midterm climate for her party. With no reliable recent general polls, the margin stays near the fundamentals-based estimate with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "Any late independent poll showing Ciscomani recovering, or a polling shift in Arizona's swing districts, could pull this back toward a tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th District sits at even partisan lean, but the 2026 midterm environment and a well-funded challenger have put first-term Republican Juan Ciscomani on the defensive. Democrat JoAnna Mendoza has raised about $11.2 million to Ciscomani's $7.6 million, and outside groups have spent heavily against both nominees. The two debated in September, and coverage through late October frames the race as one of the country's key swing contests.\n\nEvery major expert rating, from Cook and Inside Elections to the Economist and Silver Bulletin, calls the seat a Democratic flip, mostly at Lean or Likely D. Prediction markets price Mendoza's chances between 82 and 89 percent. Available polls are sparse, dated, and sponsored by partisan outfits, though each showed a narrow Democratic edge. The race remains competitive on paper, but the weight of ratings, money, and markets favors Mendoza going into Election Day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8163, "margin": 5.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8157, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8181, "margin": 5.5}, {"date":